<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Education and Learning – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 8th Grade (CEFR: A2.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Education and Learning</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Next Trimester, I Plan to Learn...</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Describe a personal challenge and resolution. Interactive: Engage in discussions about academic and future professional interests.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can describe a personal academic challenge and resolution (e.g., "I needed help with math, so I asked my teacher."). Can engage in discussions about academic interests by asking and responding to questions (e.g., "What do you like to study?" "I like science."). Can ask and answer questions about personal learning goals to express preferences and improvement areas (e.g., "What would you like to focus on improving next trimester?" "I would like to get better at writing reports.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a series of images on the board related to different academic subjects (e.g., a math textbook, a science lab, a history book, someone learning coding). Students work in pairs to quickly name the subjects and brainstorm one skill or knowledge area they associate with each. The teacher then asks students, "What subject are you most interested in learning more about next trimester?" and elicits a few answers from the class, encouraging them to use vocabulary like 'education,' 'subject,' and 'skills.'</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models sentences expressing future plans and challenges. For example, "Next trimester, I <b>would like to learn</b> more about history. I think it's a critical subject. I <b>am thinking about developing</b> my research <b>skills</b> for that. Last trimester, I <b>had</b> a challenge remembering dates in history, but I asked my classmate for help, and now I have a new strategy." The teacher clearly pronounces the modals and gerunds, exaggerating stress patterns in "I *would* like" and "I *am* thinking about learning" and highlights the /æ/ sound in "had" compared to /e/ in "head."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Am I talking about something happening now or something I want to do in the future?" (Expected answer: Future.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say 'I am thinking about developing my skills,' what part of the sentence tells you what I want to improve?" (Expected answer: 'developing my skills.')</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Education and Learning."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short audio recording (2 minutes) of two 8th-grade students (Daniel and Sofia) discussing their academic plans and challenges for the upcoming trimester. The dialogue includes expressions like "I am learning computer science," "I am interested in improving my English skills," "I would like to get better at math," "I had a challenge with reading," and "I asked my teacher." The conversation models appropriate use of target grammar (present continuous for plans, gerunds after prepositions, modals for desires) and vocabulary (career goals, develop, achieve, motivated, ambitious).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Engage in discussions about personal and learning goals and offer relevant suggestions for improvement, respecting others' opinions or thoughts.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students listen to the audio once for gist: "What is the main topic Daniel and Sofia are discussing?" (Expected answer: Their plans for next trimester.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students listen a second time and answer True/False questions (e.g., "Daniel is planning to study science next trimester. (T/F)").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students identify specific phrases in the dialogue where Daniel or Sofia discuss a challenge and its resolution, or express a goal. For example, "What subject is Sofia interested in learning more about?" or "What challenge did Daniel mention?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice using the Present continuous for future plans, Gerunds after prepositions, and Modals for desires, along with vocabulary like 'education,' 'skills,' 'knowledge,' 'career goals,' 'trimester,' 'develop,' 'achieve,' 'motivated,' and 'curious.' Focus on stress patterns in modals ("I *would* like") and minimal pairs /ɪ/ vs /iː/ (e.g., "skill" vs "feel") and /æ/ vs /e/ (e.g., "had" vs "head") during oral practice.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Guided Sentence Completion (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher provides sentence starters on the board or a handout, and students complete them orally in pairs, focusing on the target grammar. Example starters: "Next trimester, I am planning on...", "I am interested in learning about...", "To achieve my academic goals, I would like to...", "Last trimester, a challenge I had was...", "I solved it by...". Teacher monitors for accurate grammar and pronunciation (e.g., stress on "I *am* planning").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Question and Answer Chain (Semi-controlled Practice):</b> Students form a circle. The first student asks a question related to future plans or academic challenges (e.g., "What are you doing next trimester to improve your English skills, [Student B]?"). Student B answers, then asks a new question to Student C. The teacher provides a list of sentence frames for questions and answers to guide them and encourages the use of 'education,' 'knowledge,' and 'develop.' Teacher specifically prompts for clear pronunciation of 'skill' and 'head' (minimal pairs practice).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Peer Interview – "My Trimester Goals" (Pair Interaction):</b> In pairs, students interview each other using a simple questionnaire. Questions include: "What subject are you most curious about studying next trimester?", "What career goals are you developing?", "How are you planning to improve your skills in [subject]?", "What academic challenge did you have recently, and how did you resolve it?". Students use prompts like "How are you doing in [subject]?" and "You should keep practicing to get better!" to offer encouragement and ask about progress.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-17 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Education and Learning."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Role-play / "Academic Counselor" Simulation. Students work in groups of three. Two students act as classmates discussing their next trimester plans, and one student acts as an "Academic Counselor." The classmates discuss their academic and career goals, specific subjects they are interested in learning, and any challenges they anticipate or have faced, using the target vocabulary and grammar. The "Academic Counselor" listens, asks follow-up questions (e.g., "What are you doing to prepare for that course?"), offers encouragement ("You should keep practicing!"), and suggests solutions or resources (e.g., "You might be interested in developing your study skills by joining a study group").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme:</b> "Next Trimester, I Plan to Learn..." and Scenario: "Education and Learning." This task directly addresses planning for the future, discussing academic interests, describing challenges, and proposing resolutions within the school context.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency, collaborative interaction, and communicative task completion. Students are encouraged to engage in discussions about personal and learning goals and offer relevant suggestions for improvement, respecting others' opinions.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Academic Counselor" simulation, focusing on the use of target language and communicative effectiveness. A simple rubric can also be used for peer assessment.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform the "Academic Counselor" role-play, demonstrating their ability to describe personal academic challenges and resolutions, and engage in discussions about academic interests and future professional interests by asking and responding to questions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Clear stress patterns in modals and continuous forms; discernible minimal pairs where applicable (e.g., 'skill').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> (e.g., 'education,' 'skills,' 'knowledge,' 'career goals,' 'trimester,' 'develop,' 'achieve').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and coherence:</b> Expressing ideas clearly, describing challenges and resolutions logically.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaborative interaction:</b> Actively listening, asking relevant questions, and offering appropriate suggestions or encouragement.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two sentences orally or in writing:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today, I feel confident about discussing... (e.g., my next trimester plans / academic challenges)."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I would like to get better at... (e.g., asking follow-up questions / describing my solutions)."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new vocabulary or grammar did I use most effectively in my conversations today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did I help my classmates in our group discussions, and how did they help me?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b> The "Academic Counselor" simulation worked well for encouraging natural interaction and the use of all target language. Students were motivated to share their future plans and offer advice. Some students still need more practice with stress patterns in modals, especially "I *would* like." I also noticed that while they used many vocabulary words, some struggled to correctly form gerunds after prepositions in spontaneous speech. For the next lesson (Writing Composition), I will scaffold sentence construction by providing sentence frames and focusing on correct gerund forms in writing, drawing on the speaking practice from today.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> The speaking activities in this lesson, particularly discussing academic goals and challenges, will directly prepare students for Lesson 4 (Writing Composition), where they will write a short paragraph or reflection about their own future academic goals, including challenges and proposed solutions.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>